Hey!

51
00:01:44,840 --> 00:01:46,320
Welcome back to the Scoutmaster Podcast.

52
00:01:46,940 --> 00:01:48,440
This is Clark Green.

53
00:01:49,320 --> 00:01:52,060
Don't know about you, but it's been pretty busy around here.

54
00:01:53,040 --> 00:01:53,240
Hey!

55
00:01:53,240 --> 00:01:58,220
It's the fall. Camping weekends. Going backpacking soon. Love to go backpacking.

56
00:01:59,360 --> 00:02:02,280
So the emails keep pouring in, as do the comments on the blog.

57
00:02:03,119 --> 00:02:09,500
Now some of them I'm going to hold on to because they relate to a subject that we're going to talk about later on.

58
00:02:10,280 --> 00:02:18,480
But here's one that I got from Chris, who's a Cub Master in Sylvania, Ohio.

59
00:02:18,480 --> 00:02:25,320
Mr. Green. Oh, you don't need to call me Mr. Chris. You can call me Clark.

60
00:02:26,079 --> 00:02:31,540
Thank you for your great podcast. I enjoy listening to your jokes and the scout information you give out.

61
00:02:32,640 --> 00:02:35,839
Well, there's another person that enjoys listening to the jokes.

62
00:02:36,700 --> 00:02:39,100
I'm beginning to worry about you people, to tell you the truth.

63
00:02:40,060 --> 00:02:44,579
Well, on today's podcast, we're going to be talking about something called Contribution Syndrome.

64
00:02:46,579 --> 00:02:47,859
Contribution Syndrome.

65
00:02:48,480 --> 00:02:54,959
It's a pretty good concept. It's something that I found looking around and I'm going to share it with you.

66
00:02:55,560 --> 00:02:56,560
I'm going to contribute.

67
00:02:59,380 --> 00:03:01,920
It's all part of the syndrome. We'll talk about that in a moment.

68
00:03:02,359 --> 00:03:03,880
Then we're going to talk a little bit about hazing.

69
00:03:04,160 --> 00:03:10,480
A little bit more of a sober subject, but something that we need to know, understand, and know how to approach as Scoutmasters.

70
00:03:11,280 --> 00:03:16,160
And that'll about do it for this podcast. So let's get started, shall we?

71
00:03:16,160 --> 00:03:20,520
Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less.

72
00:03:20,520 --> 00:03:33,500
I'm reading to you from David Axon's book, A-X-S-O-N, if you want to look it up.

73
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:36,600
The Management Mythbuster.

74
00:03:36,600 --> 00:03:43,620
Now, he asserts that successful leaders are successful in part because they ask great questions.

75
00:03:43,620 --> 00:03:48,420
Now, I'm a big fan of Scoutmasters asking questions and not doing a lot of talking.

76
00:03:48,980 --> 00:03:54,560
Because when you ask questions and you ask the right questions, Scouts learn to think for themselves.

77
00:03:54,720 --> 00:03:56,079
They solve their own problems.

78
00:03:56,080 --> 00:03:59,000
They think things through and they get in that habit.

79
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:01,440
And boy, I'll tell you, it works wonderfully.

80
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:06,420
It's a great tool for every Scoutmaster to have asking good questions.

81
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:09,140
Problem is, is I know that I talk too much.

82
00:04:11,220 --> 00:04:12,480
I know I talk.

83
00:04:12,580 --> 00:04:13,640
I have a podcast.

84
00:04:13,920 --> 00:04:15,540
How can I not talk too much?

85
00:04:15,720 --> 00:04:19,840
I see a fair number of Scout leaders who probably talk a little bit too much, too.

86
00:04:19,839 --> 00:04:29,659
After reading David Axon's description of Contribution Syndrome, I've diagnosed myself and I know I've got it.

87
00:04:29,700 --> 00:04:31,039
I've got Contribution Syndrome.

88
00:04:31,700 --> 00:04:32,979
See if this fits for you.

89
00:04:33,479 --> 00:04:34,340
Let me read it to you.

90
00:04:34,739 --> 00:04:35,679
Contribution Syndrome.

91
00:04:37,080 --> 00:04:38,099
This is what it means.

92
00:04:38,159 --> 00:04:45,159
It means no matter what the situation, you have the compulsion to contribute and to show how smart you are at every possible opportunity.

93
00:04:45,160 --> 00:04:50,240
Our whole merit system is based on getting the answer right.

94
00:04:51,360 --> 00:04:57,920
As we move into the business world, Contribution Syndrome can infect our every poor.

95
00:04:58,520 --> 00:05:01,860
We must be seen to be contributing at every meeting or on every project.

96
00:05:02,520 --> 00:05:08,080
For some, this manifests itself in a complete inability to sit quietly and listen.

97
00:05:08,620 --> 00:05:12,460
It seems to be part of their DNA to feel that if they're not talking, they're not working.

98
00:05:12,460 --> 00:05:16,299
Now, the best leaders, Axon goes on to say, stop talking.

99
00:05:16,400 --> 00:05:25,640
They sit quietly, taking in the contribution of everyone else and limiting their own contribution to asking a few pertinent questions that lead to discussion in a constructive manner.

100
00:05:26,460 --> 00:05:28,520
Great questions are not complex questions.

101
00:05:30,679 --> 00:05:34,940
In fact, the best seem to be blindingly obvious questions.

102
00:05:34,940 --> 00:05:44,140
Unfortunately, a relatively small proportion of people in leadership positions have the courage, confidence, or even the basic common sense to ask the right questions.

103
00:05:44,719 --> 00:05:48,780
The most potent question anyone can ever ask is this simple three-letter inquiry.

104
00:05:49,640 --> 00:05:49,980
Why?

105
00:05:50,540 --> 00:05:55,020
The key is to keep asking dumb but great questions.

106
00:05:55,740 --> 00:05:59,580
This demonstrates a level of humility that is essential for effective leadership.

107
00:06:00,780 --> 00:06:03,960
Complexity has become synonymous with sophistication.

108
00:06:03,960 --> 00:06:05,719
That's a really good statement.

109
00:06:06,719 --> 00:06:07,539
Let me read it again.

110
00:06:08,799 --> 00:06:13,460
Complexity has become synonymous with sophistication.

111
00:06:13,859 --> 00:06:16,440
How do we get better at asking great questions?

112
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:18,000
We practice.

113
00:06:18,820 --> 00:06:21,640
And that's all from David Axon's book there.

114
00:06:22,539 --> 00:06:27,620
I think we're all scout masters and scout leaders for a reason.

115
00:06:28,060 --> 00:06:30,099
We want to help youth.

116
00:06:30,100 --> 00:06:33,160
We want to educate, inform, mentor them.

117
00:06:33,820 --> 00:06:38,280
And one of the main ways you do that is you open your mouth and you talk to them.

118
00:06:39,180 --> 00:06:40,760
And you talk and talk and talk.

119
00:06:41,400 --> 00:06:44,200
And I find myself doing that quite often.

120
00:06:44,340 --> 00:06:47,480
I can watch the blood drain out of their faces.

121
00:06:47,480 --> 00:06:55,760
I'm standing there going on and on and on and telling them what I think and how this should be done and how that should be done.

122
00:06:56,380 --> 00:07:04,220
If I manage to catch myself and I switch to asking questions, then I can see the excitement and engagement in their faces.

123
00:07:04,220 --> 00:07:09,740
I can see them start to join in the game and start to try and figure things out.

124
00:07:11,060 --> 00:07:13,160
And that's really what we want to be doing.

125
00:07:13,740 --> 00:07:17,560
We really want to work with them and get them to start thinking.

126
00:07:18,020 --> 00:07:22,180
To get them to start thinking and figuring things out for themselves.

127
00:07:24,480 --> 00:07:25,140
Axon said,

128
00:07:25,140 --> 00:07:48,539
The courage part is knowing that what you have to say, while it might be important and it might be salient and related to what's at hand, is not necessarily the best course of action.

129
00:07:49,360 --> 00:07:52,039
Well, that confidence is not necessarily in yourself.

130
00:07:52,040 --> 00:08:05,240
It's the confidence in your scouts that they are going to be able to come up with good answers, with good plans, with good ideas, without having to hear them from you.

131
00:08:06,900 --> 00:08:15,340
And common sense, well, if you're working with scout age boys, it's not common sense to talk to them for 15, 20 minutes at a clip.

132
00:08:15,800 --> 00:08:18,180
Not common sense to lecture them.

133
00:08:18,220 --> 00:08:18,920
It doesn't work.

134
00:08:18,920 --> 00:08:21,140
It just plain won't work.

135
00:08:22,040 --> 00:08:23,960
The best leaders, he said, stop talking.

136
00:08:24,160 --> 00:08:34,620
They sit quietly, taking in the contributions of everyone else and limiting their own contribution to asking a few pertinent questions that lead the discussion in a constructive manner.

137
00:08:35,460 --> 00:08:42,360
One good way to sit quietly and get the contributions from scouts is to leave the room.

138
00:08:42,360 --> 00:08:50,900
Now, we've talked about this rather extensively in the past, but I'll remind you that your very presence changes the dynamics of everything.

139
00:08:51,900 --> 00:08:54,260
Scouts have been taught in school.

140
00:08:54,440 --> 00:08:55,640
They've been taught at home.

141
00:08:55,760 --> 00:08:56,860
They've been taught in church.

142
00:08:56,860 --> 00:09:03,320
They've been taught in the sports teams that they participate in that when an adult is there, the adult is the leader.

143
00:09:03,880 --> 00:09:05,300
The adult has the answers.

144
00:09:05,780 --> 00:09:07,700
The adult will be directing things.

145
00:09:08,520 --> 00:09:13,840
So you need to be out of the way and let them figure stuff out and then bring that to you.

146
00:09:14,120 --> 00:09:19,420
Larry Geiger said something like that in a comment he left because this was the subject of a blog post.

147
00:09:19,420 --> 00:09:23,760
He said this, one way to hear scouts is to isolate yourselves from them completely.

148
00:09:24,540 --> 00:09:31,339
Each year at our annual planning conference, I put them in a room for two hours and then all the adults leave.

149
00:09:32,060 --> 00:09:32,740
Their assignment?

150
00:09:33,260 --> 00:09:34,680
To create an annual calendar.

151
00:09:35,140 --> 00:09:39,120
The senior patrol leader gives them the standard BSA planning sheet and they go to work.

152
00:09:39,860 --> 00:09:42,920
Now they have to listen to each other and not me.

153
00:09:42,920 --> 00:09:49,839
Build in physical separation, he says, and that will build interaction between your scouts and they'll start thinking.

154
00:09:50,300 --> 00:09:51,800
They'll start figuring things out.

155
00:09:52,180 --> 00:09:55,640
You know, the other piece of this is asking the right questions.

156
00:09:56,380 --> 00:10:04,880
Yes, but it's also listening and listening very carefully because if you don't listen carefully, you're not going to know to ask the right questions.

157
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:06,920
You don't know what's going on.

158
00:10:06,920 --> 00:10:10,860
Not to think ahead of everything.

159
00:10:11,040 --> 00:10:17,560
Not to leapfrog over what they're saying into the next contribution that you're going to make.

160
00:10:18,640 --> 00:10:19,880
Now, I think it's some good thinking.

161
00:10:20,079 --> 00:10:22,920
I think Axon has got it pegged right there.

162
00:10:23,400 --> 00:10:25,680
Now, he's writing to management people.

163
00:10:25,839 --> 00:10:28,560
He's writing about business leadership and things like that.

164
00:10:28,560 --> 00:10:37,439
I'm not a big fan of applying a lot of management science to scouting because I think there's a point past which it works with scouting.

165
00:10:38,060 --> 00:10:47,920
But every once in a while, you can come up with a little piece like Axon's contribution syndrome that works very, very well.

166
00:10:48,280 --> 00:10:54,020
And I hope that it'll inspire you and that you'll find it as useful as I have.

167
00:10:58,560 --> 00:11:15,680
So we were out backpacking one time.

168
00:11:15,819 --> 00:11:19,140
We had a load of scouts and six adults going along.

169
00:11:19,319 --> 00:11:22,959
And it ran on into the evening and the adults were sitting around the campfire.

170
00:11:23,619 --> 00:11:25,020
We were kind of reminiscing.

171
00:11:25,020 --> 00:11:31,600
Now, out of that six, three had either never been in scouts or had only been in scouts for a short time.

172
00:11:32,780 --> 00:11:39,160
The two who had been in scouts for a brief time said they left scouting because they were being bullied or hazed.

173
00:11:40,439 --> 00:11:46,079
And that's two out of three who had been in just for a brief amount of time.

174
00:11:46,800 --> 00:11:48,380
What is hazing exactly?

175
00:11:48,380 --> 00:11:58,260
I mean, hazing can be a ritual or other activities that use harassment, abuse, or humiliation as a way of initiating a person into a group.

176
00:11:58,380 --> 00:12:00,980
That's one way hazing can be looked at.

177
00:12:01,980 --> 00:12:09,900
It can be expressed in practical jokes, in bullying, in unwanted physical contact, or a number of different methods.

178
00:12:09,900 --> 00:12:17,800
I think what we need to understand at the get-go, and I think you probably already understand this, is all hazing.

179
00:12:19,360 --> 00:12:22,620
All hazing is antithetical to the spirit of scouting.

180
00:12:24,900 --> 00:12:26,360
Well, how do we know if it's hazing?

181
00:12:26,540 --> 00:12:35,000
If an activity or a practice or a tradition is intended to humiliate or trick or abuse or harass, it's hazing.

182
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:38,720
And you've got to put these things to a stop immediately.

183
00:12:40,400 --> 00:12:45,880
Some of these things may even be a hallowed tradition in your scout troop.

184
00:12:46,460 --> 00:12:47,840
But it's got to be stopped.

185
00:12:48,300 --> 00:12:49,480
And it's got to be eliminated.

186
00:12:50,340 --> 00:12:58,760
I can tell you, as a younger scoutmaster, you know, in my mid-20s, I really wasn't very savvy about this.

187
00:12:58,760 --> 00:13:13,640
And I thought little of the detrimental effects of hazing, and I even participated in some of the standard practical jokes that were all too common at the time and are thankfully becoming increasingly rare, like snipe hunts and things like that.

188
00:13:13,640 --> 00:13:27,780
It embarrasses me that adults, some adults in scouting, seem to be unaware of the immense power differential between them and their scouts.

189
00:13:28,240 --> 00:13:33,020
And they exploit that difference of power to humiliate a bunch of boys.

190
00:13:33,960 --> 00:13:35,800
I just find that embarrassing.

191
00:13:36,800 --> 00:13:41,840
As with most things, kindness is the antidote.

192
00:13:41,840 --> 00:13:44,800
Now, kindness isn't weakness.

193
00:13:45,540 --> 00:13:50,400
Kindness isn't meaning that we're lacking discipline or intensity.

194
00:13:50,660 --> 00:13:51,640
It's just being kind.

195
00:13:52,900 --> 00:13:56,120
Instead of being a source of frustration, we should be a source of support.

196
00:13:57,160 --> 00:14:04,880
I mean, scoutmasters aren't supposed to antagonize their scouts, but to lend them confidence, to help them find their own talents and strengths.

197
00:14:06,180 --> 00:14:09,140
If you look in the Guide to Safe Scouting, it'll say this.

198
00:14:09,140 --> 00:14:15,880
Any form of hazing, initiations, ridicule, or inappropriate teasing are prohibited and should not be allowed.

199
00:14:15,880 --> 00:14:28,640
And if you want to go to the dictionary, hazing is defined as exacting unnecessary, disagreeable, or difficult work.

200
00:14:29,260 --> 00:14:36,620
It's defined as harassing or annoying by playing abusive or shameful tricks on a person.

201
00:14:36,620 --> 00:14:41,240
Or to humiliate them by playing practical jokes on them.

202
00:14:42,460 --> 00:14:50,020
And the dictionary notes that this is used especially of college students as the sophomore hazed a freshman.

203
00:14:50,020 --> 00:14:59,780
A scoutmaster can do no greater service to his troop, in my opinion, than to make sure that every vestige of hazing is gone.

204
00:15:00,579 --> 00:15:04,740
I mean, initiations, teasing, compelling scouts to humiliate themselves in any way.

205
00:15:05,360 --> 00:15:08,060
This quite simply has no place in scouting.

206
00:15:08,060 --> 00:15:13,540
When a boy joins a scout troop, he hasn't got anything to prove to anyone.

207
00:15:14,079 --> 00:15:17,300
He is immediately and unreservedly accepted.

208
00:15:18,119 --> 00:15:21,900
If his behavior has been bad, he's properly corrected.

209
00:15:22,579 --> 00:15:27,560
If he does not know what to do, he's treated generously and kindly.

210
00:15:27,560 --> 00:15:33,719
Now, Kyle read some of this information on the blog and he sent me a story.

211
00:15:34,540 --> 00:15:42,859
He says, I hope that hazing is a lot less prevalent in scouting than it was during my time as a scout in the mid-70s.

212
00:15:43,920 --> 00:15:48,079
Hazing was kind of a daily fact of life on campouts and at scout summer camp.

213
00:15:48,719 --> 00:15:53,719
And he goes on to explain a pretty horrific experience.

214
00:15:53,720 --> 00:16:02,960
And you can only imagine being 11, 12, 13 years old and having to go through some kind of a gauntlet

215
00:16:02,960 --> 00:16:07,140
or being treated as though you were a new recruit in the army

216
00:16:07,140 --> 00:16:12,180
and having a patrol leader or a senior patrol leader or, worse yet, an adult

217
00:16:12,180 --> 00:16:18,520
who wants to be your new drill sergeant, who wants to holler at you and berate you.

218
00:16:19,500 --> 00:16:20,900
Kyle goes on to say,

219
00:16:20,900 --> 00:16:26,900
I was always at least as interested in the character training involved in scouting as I was in the camping skills.

220
00:16:27,800 --> 00:16:30,980
And what my experiences unfortunately taught me about character

221
00:16:30,980 --> 00:16:36,060
was there were a lot of people who were attracted to opportunities to wear uniforms

222
00:16:36,060 --> 00:16:39,240
and exercise power over other people for all the wrong reasons.

223
00:16:40,240 --> 00:16:46,819
I hope scouting has grown in its ability to identify hazing and to eliminate it.

224
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:49,100
Well, Kyle, I think it really has.

225
00:16:49,100 --> 00:16:51,260
I'm sorry you had such a bad experience.

226
00:16:52,140 --> 00:16:56,620
And it's very, very saddening, even all these years later.

227
00:16:57,000 --> 00:17:02,280
We don't want any of our scouts speaking of us in that wise, do we, fellas, ladies?

228
00:17:03,080 --> 00:17:04,700
No. No.

229
00:17:04,940 --> 00:17:08,780
So we're not going to allow hazing in our troops.

230
00:17:09,540 --> 00:17:12,099
Larry Geiger weighed in on the subject as well.

231
00:17:12,300 --> 00:17:12,599
He says,

232
00:17:12,599 --> 00:17:34,260
He talks about going on a camp out as a scout and there was a bit of a hazing incident.

233
00:17:34,260 --> 00:17:37,700
And there was something about they had to go out and sit in the middle of the woods.

234
00:17:37,980 --> 00:17:41,220
And he woke up, he got tired of the whole thing.

235
00:17:41,960 --> 00:17:43,280
So Larry went to bed.

236
00:17:43,760 --> 00:17:45,240
He went to his tent.

237
00:17:45,780 --> 00:17:52,040
You know, when he woke up in the morning, he looked around and there was a whole lot of action going on

238
00:17:52,040 --> 00:17:59,159
